  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-216212",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line $3x+y=1.$ \n\nTo find the slope of the line $3x+y=1,$ we convert the equation to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y & =1 \\\\ y & =-3x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the slope-intercept form, we can tell that the slope of the line is $-3.$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $-3$ that passes through the point $(1,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-3(x-1) \\\\ y-1 & =-3x+3 \\\\ y & =-3x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write this equation in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ 3x+y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
